[
    {
        "content": "<p>Is the following result in mathlib?  I'm surprised I couldn't find it anywhere. <code>inferInstance</code> doesn't work, either.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Monoid</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">mul</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">++</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">mul_assoc</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">append_assoc</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">one</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">one_mul</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">nil_append</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">mul_one</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">append_nil</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 618103354,
        "sender_full_name": "Ching-Tsun Chou",
        "timestamp": 1787412379
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>see <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=FreeMonoid#doc\">docs#FreeMonoid</a></p>",
        "id": 618103651,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1787412626
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks!  But where is it proved that a <code>FreeMonoid</code> is a <code>Monoid</code>?</p>",
        "id": 618104916,
        "sender_full_name": "Ching-Tsun Chou",
        "timestamp": 1787413715
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Algebra/FreeMonoid/Basic.html#FreeMonoid.instCancelMonoid\">https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Algebra/FreeMonoid/Basic.html#FreeMonoid.instCancelMonoid</a></p>",
        "id": 618104961,
        "sender_full_name": "Weiyi Wang",
        "timestamp": 1787413765
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Ah, I see.  Thanks!</p>",
        "id": 618105149,
        "sender_full_name": "Ching-Tsun Chou",
        "timestamp": 1787413891
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>But, still, <code>CancelMonoid (FreeMonoid α)</code> implies <code>Monoid (FreeMonoid α)</code>, not <code>Monoid (List α)</code> or <code>CancelMonoid (List α)</code>.  To be sure, <code>FreeMonoid α</code> and <code>List α</code> are <code>Equiv</code>, but that falls short of saying that they are isomorphic as monoids, doesn't it?  If I want to use <code>List α</code> as a monoid, I still have to prove it manually, don't I?</p>",
        "id": 618105810,
        "sender_full_name": "Ching-Tsun Chou",
        "timestamp": 1787414426
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Why do you want to use <code>List</code> as a monoid?</p>",
        "id": 618106601,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1787415095
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Does it have to be specifically <code>List</code> for some reason? Why can't you just use <code>FreeMonoid</code> instead?</p>",
        "id": 618106625,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1787415115
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Because I want to talk about homomorphism between languages (in the CS sense):<br>\n<a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Computability/Language.html\">https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Computability/Language.html</a><br>\nwhich are defined on <code>List α</code>.  Computer scientists talk about lists, not free monoids!</p>",
        "id": 618106885,
        "sender_full_name": "Ching-Tsun Chou",
        "timestamp": 1787415408
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Can you elaborate on where the need for the Monoid structure shows up?</p>",
        "id": 618107223,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1787415708
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I want to prove that the image and inverse image of a regular language are still regular under a \"homomorphism\", which is really a monoid homomorphism when <code>List α</code> is viewed as a monoid.</p>",
        "id": 618107456,
        "sender_full_name": "Ching-Tsun Chou",
        "timestamp": 1787415928
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Here's the definition of a \"homomorphism\" from Dexter Kozen's textbook:<br>\n<img alt=\"image.png\" class=\"inline-image\" data-original-content-type=\"image/png\" data-original-dimensions=\"1227x397\" data-original-src=\"/user_uploads/3121/EmtxL48r-KE8AqKynDUoe-8P/image.png\" src=\"/user_uploads/thumbnail/3121/EmtxL48r-KE8AqKynDUoe-8P/image.png/840x560.webp\"></p>",
        "id": 618107556,
        "sender_full_name": "Ching-Tsun Chou",
        "timestamp": 1787416022
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>It is a monoid homomorphism, isn't it?</p>",
        "id": 618107612,
        "sender_full_name": "Ching-Tsun Chou",
        "timestamp": 1787416067
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I don't mind proving <code>Monoid (List α)</code> myself, but it seems rather strange that this result is not in mathlib already.</p>",
        "id": 618107690,
        "sender_full_name": "Ching-Tsun Chou",
        "timestamp": 1787416147
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Since a homomorphism is exactly determined by its action on the singletons could you maybe just take an ordinary function <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ</mi><mo>→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Sigma \\to \\Gamma^*</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">Σ</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6887em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">Γ</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6887em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mbin mtight\">∗</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>? The action on lists is given by <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=List.flatMap#doc\">docs#List.flatMap</a>.</p>",
        "id": 618107775,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1787416232
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>That's not the most natural definition, when we have the perfectly good monoid homomorphism notion to use.</p>",
        "id": 618107944,
        "sender_full_name": "Ching-Tsun Chou",
        "timestamp": 1787416381
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>There is an open PR to redefine <code>Language</code> in terms of <code>FreeMonoid</code>, at which point you wouldn't need to talk about <code>List</code></p>",
        "id": 618121884,
        "sender_full_name": "Yaël Dillies",
        "timestamp": 1787425889
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>What is that PR?</p>\n<p>I'm not sure that's a good idea.  <code>Language</code> is mostly used by computer scientists and most computer scientists are not taught what a \"free monoid\" is.  Defining <code>Language</code> in terms of <code>FreeMonoid</code> just creates confusion without adding anything of mathematical substance.  Indeed, if you look at the code of <code>FreeMonoid</code>, it's literally just an alias for <code>List</code>:<br>\n<a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/b78b57f2047c2f441a7ce779a951cd9db0258a6d/Mathlib/Algebra/FreeMonoid/Basic.lean#L65-L66\">https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/b78b57f2047c2f441a7ce779a951cd9db0258a6d/Mathlib/Algebra/FreeMonoid/Basic.lean#L65-L66</a><br>\nand most theorems in that file are proved using theorems about <code>List</code> (or literally direct translations of theorems about <code>List</code>).  Furthermore, why stop at <code>Language</code>?  Why not change <code>List</code> to <code>FreeMonoid</code> everywhere?</p>",
        "id": 618125133,
        "sender_full_name": "Ching-Tsun Chou",
        "timestamp": 1787429042
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>IMHO, if you want to improve mathlib's <code>Language</code>, you should get the PR <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/36934\">mathlib#36934</a> merged first.  That PR has been open for 5 months and actually does something meaningful.</p>",
        "id": 618125449,
        "sender_full_name": "Ching-Tsun Chou",
        "timestamp": 1787429310
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Well the problem is that there is not just one reasonable <code>Monoid</code> structure on <code>List</code>s. For example, you could also consider elementwise multiplication as a monoid where <code>(1 : List Nat) = [1]</code> and <code>[2, 6] * [3, 4] = [6, 8, 18, 24]</code>. Furthermore, you might want to instead have an additive structure on lists and if we had both it would be even more confusing. Instead, if you want lists with concatenation as multiplication / addition, you should use <code>FreeMonoid</code> / <code>FreeAddMonoid</code>.</p>",
        "id": 618128373,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1787431888
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"110637\">@Ching-Tsun Chou</span> I agree with the other messages in the thread: this is a common idiom whenever there are multiple reasonable instances for the same type. It's analogous to why we have both <code>Monoid</code> and <code>AddMonoid</code>. Imagine the confusion if these were combined and one class needed to handle both the additive and multiplicative cases for say the natural numbers. It may also be desirable for <code>FreeMonoid</code> to be a one-field structure, but this is an independent issue.</p>",
        "id": 618130632,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris Henson",
        "timestamp": 1787434177
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>My problem is how do I define a \"homomorphism\" between languages by leveraging the monoid structure of <code>List</code> under <code>++</code> and <code>[]</code>.  The most straightforward approach that occurs to me is to prove <code>Monoid (List α)</code> (or the stronger <code>CancelMonoid (List α)</code> and then say a \"homomorphism\" is a monoid homomorphism under this monoid structure.  But I seem to be told that this is not a good idea.  I don't really understand why this is not a good idea.  That there is an alternative monoid structure on <code>List</code> doesn't mean <code>Monoid (List α)</code> is not true, does it? (Indeed, my <code>example</code> at the beginning proves it.)  Why can't I use a true fact?</p>",
        "id": 618131363,
        "sender_full_name": "Ching-Tsun Chou",
        "timestamp": 1787434930
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Another approach is to just define a \"homomorphism\" exactly as in Dexter Kozen's textbook without pointing out the connection to monoids at all.  But is that a good idea?</p>",
        "id": 618131701,
        "sender_full_name": "Ching-Tsun Chou",
        "timestamp": 1787435297
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"110637\">Ching-Tsun Chou</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/217875-Is-there-code-for-X.3F/topic/List.20as.20a.20monoid/near/618131363\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>That there is an alternative monoid structure on <code>List</code> doesn't mean <code>Monoid (List α)</code> is not true, does it? (Indeed, my <code>example</code> at the beginning proves it.) Why can't I use a true fact?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><code>Monoid (List α)</code> is not a proposition, so it can't be true or false. It is true that <code>Nonempty (Monoid (List α))</code>, but that probably doesn't help you.</p>\n<p>I don't understand the opposition to <code>FreeMonoid</code>, but you can probably have a <code>def</code> for <code>Monoid (List α)</code> using concatenation, or perhaps a <code>scoped instance</code>.</p>\n<p>e.g.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">@[</span><span class=\"n\">implicit_reducible</span><span class=\"kd\">]</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">appendCancelMonoid</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">CancelMonoid</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">inferInstanceAs</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">CancelMonoid</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">FreeMonoid</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 618131899,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1787435513
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"933054\">@Snir Broshi</span>   I'm not opposed to <code>FreeMonoid</code>.  I just don't know how it can help me if I'm not even supposed to talk about <code>Monoid (List α)</code> or <code>CancelMonoid (List α)</code>.  At least that's my impression from reading the comments above.  If my impression is wrong, please let me know.  It's not hard to prove <code>CancelMonoid (List α)</code> even without using <code>FreeMonoid</code>.</p>",
        "id": 618132584,
        "sender_full_name": "Ching-Tsun Chou",
        "timestamp": 1787436071
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"110637\">Ching-Tsun Chou</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/217875-Is-there-code-for-X.3F/topic/List.20as.20a.20monoid/near/618132584\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I'm not even supposed to talk about <code>Monoid (List α)</code> or <code>CancelMonoid (List α)</code></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>If you create a <code>def</code> or a <code>scoped instance</code> then I think it's fine. But creating a global <code>instance</code> for it would mean that you're forcing a specific choice of <code>Monoid</code> on lists for whoever imports your code, which isn't great.</p>\n<p>For <code>(Σ Γ : Type*)</code> you can talk about <code>FreeMonoid Σ →* FreeMonoid Γ</code>, which is the type of monoid homomorphisms from <code>List Σ</code> to <code>List Γ</code> where the monoid action on both sides is appending lists.<br>\nRight now <code>FreeMonoid</code> is a \"type-alias\" for <code>List</code>s (as you've noticed), so everything works great.<br>\nIf Mathlib decides that lists are an implementation detail then they might change the definition of <code>FreeMonoid</code> at any point, so using it might not be a good idea, but I think strings in CS are free monoids, so even if the underlying representation changes you're good.</p>\n<p>I think you can either:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Use <code>FreeMonoid α</code></li>\n<li>Create your own type-alias for <code>List</code>s and define a global <code>instance : CancelMonoid (Foo α)</code></li>\n<li>Create a <code>scoped instance : CancelMonoid (List α)</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>All 3 are probably fine, and the 3rd option is probably easiest to understand since there's no type-alias involved.<br>\nThe difference is semantics: are you really working with lists, or with a free monoid that just happens to be implemented by lists (but could be e.g. <code>String</code>, or a custom structure containing a list), or with something else entirely?</p>",
        "id": 618134814,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1787437851
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>one compromise could be defining your own type of \"language homomorphisms\", which under the hood are monoid homs but come with a <code>FunLike</code> instance on lists</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">LanguageHom</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">β</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">FreeMonoid</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">FreeMonoid</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">β</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">β</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">FunLike</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">LanguageHom</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">β</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">β</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">...</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>not sure if that addresses your concerns</p>",
        "id": 618178623,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Waring",
        "timestamp": 1787475241
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><del>What is the difference between <code>MonoidHom</code> and <code>MonoidHomClass</code> in mathlib?</del><br>\nNever mind.  I figured that out.</p>",
        "id": 618220172,
        "sender_full_name": "Ching-Tsun Chou",
        "timestamp": 1787510405
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Using <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"931332\">@Thomas Waring</span>'s idea, I got the following code, which seems quite nice:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">namespace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Language</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">open</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">FreeMonoid</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">β</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">abbrev</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Hom</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">β</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">FreeMonoid</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">FreeMonoid</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">β</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">scoped</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">FunLike</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Hom</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">β</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">β</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">coe</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Hom</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">β</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">toList</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∘</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∘</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ofList</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">coe_injective</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">intro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">ext</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">exact</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">funext_iff</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">mp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">namespace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Hom</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Hom</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">β</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">map_one</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">exact</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MonoidHom</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">map_one</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">map_mul</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">as</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">as'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">as</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">++</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">as'</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">as</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">++</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">as'</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">exact</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MonoidHom</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">map_mul</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">as</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">as'</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">end</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Hom</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">end</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Language</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
